A||||10||WisconsinGT||Oil leak....Help!||||1
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||11-10-1999||08:51 PM||||I am noticing a small puddle of oil under my 88GT. It is under or around the left rear tire. I noticed after taking off the rear tire that I see oil by the splash gard. Anyone have any ideas where it could be coming from on that side of the car.Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-11-1999||01:09 AM||||the only thing there...... the tranny. check the cooling lines if it's an auto. one of them may be leaking.<P>If they are you need to use tranny rated hydraulic line to repair it. Not fuel line like some people do. You can find this on the shelf with the tranny coolers at pep boys etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||11-11-1999||09:27 AM||||Thanks Orge. It is not the transmission. The oil is clear or light brown in color. Also, when I checked the oil, There was very little. I am guessing that it is the oil pan. I do not think there is anything on that side of the car that could be oil related.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||11-11-1999||10:29 AM||||Plus sometimes oil will leak and run along things before it starts to drip.  You should put the car on ramps or jack stands, and spray it with a cleaner/degreaser and then give it another day to reappear.  That way you know for sure where you are leaking from. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||11-11-1999||10:43 AM||||You may have been there already but I would look at the distributor base, is the O-ring bad and leaking. After 10+ years they get hard and do not seal anything. Not sure how the oil is getting over to the wheel, but at least this is on that side of the motor? I thought my valve covers were leaking and it was this dumb O-ring. Good luck.||||reg||3
Z||000005||wwh0001||11-11-1999||01:40 PM||||If the oil is "brownish" and next to the wheel. look in the wheel or on the side of the tire. could be a leaking caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||11-11-1999||02:09 PM||||Another thought. Considering all the options we have for tranny fluid (oil) it could be an axel seal? Just thinking with the keyboard?||||reg||5
Z||000007||theogre||11-11-1999||05:12 PM||||assuming it's not motor oil running allong the cradle......<P>old tranny fluid can be clear to light brown in color. tranny fluid that's not real old can look clear/brownish when spread thin. The red dye fades over time.<P>the tranny cooler lines go to the left strut tower before heading to the front. You could also be leaking strut oil which is also usually clear.<P>Of course manual trannys use engine oil, so that's going to fit the description too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||11-11-1999||08:50 PM||||Thanks all for your answers. I forgot to add that I see oil on the top of my valve cover where the PCV Valve resides. I am very confused.||||reg||5
Z||000009||DJRice||11-12-1999||09:31 AM||||Thats probably blow-by.  But if its making that big of a mess, then you may have a bigger fish to fry.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||11-12-1999||01:17 PM||||oil around the PCV fittings is likely the PCV grommit or line hardware getting cruddy. replace the offending part. This could be leaking enough to drop oil on the ground but its got to really bad to do that. Even with no blowby, the PCV grommits and other valve cover parts gets enough oil sprayed at them to leak if they're old.||||reg||1
